2. BSP Kernel and Distros
=========================

The following table summarizes the valid Wind River Linux distros for this BSP.
'Y' in each content cell stands for supported; 'N' stands for not supported:

  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  | valid/distro |   wrlinux   | wrlinux-graphics | wrlinux-installer | wrlinux-tiny |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  |    valid     |      Y      |        Y         |         N         |       N      |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+

For the supported kernel type for this BSP, please check the TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES
by running 'bitbake -e virtual/kernel | grep "^TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES="'.


3. Board Specific Patches
=========================

To get a list of patches applied to the kernel specific to this BSP along with
patch descriptions use git-whatchanged on the default kernel. For example:

  $ cd tmp/work-shared/<bsp_name>/kernel-source
  $ git whatchanged v6.18/standard/base..v6.18/standard/nvidia-soc


4. Build Instructions
=====================

4.1 Basic feautre BSP
---------------------

This basic BSP only supports some common devices. For example, network, USB, eMMC and so on.

4.1.1 Setup BSP project
-----------------------

	--machine nvidia-orin-agx --distro wrlinux-graphics --dl-layers --all-layers --accept-eula=yes

4.1.2 Setup environment and create a build directory
----------------------------------------------------

	$ . environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux
	$ . oe-init-build-env [<build>]

4.1.3 Build project
-------------------

Build project with below command:

	bitbake wrlinux-image-std-sato

After building project completely, system booting images are in <project path>/build/tmp/deploy/images/<MACHINE>.
User needs to boot these images from network, please refer to detailed steps in section 6.2.

4.2 Full feautre BSP
--------------------

If users want to enable NVIDIA specific feature like CUDA, deepstream, tensorRT and so on, it needs to add
additional packages into Yocto project. But because these packages are under the NVIDIA Proprietary license,
they are not able to be integrated into WRLinux product by default. Based on this reason, users need to build
NVIDIA BSP with network enabled environment so that these NVIDIA Proprietary packages are able to be downloaded
during building project. About the detailed building steps, please refer to section 1 in README.proprietary.

Note: If users want to integrate NVIDIA Proprietary package into their own product,
      they MUST consult NVIDIA and their own legal department.

5. Deploy Instructions
======================

5.1 Create Deploying Environment
--------------------------------

	Create deploying environment with below commands:

	$ mkdir nvidia-deploy
	$ cp <build output path>/wrlinux-image-std-sato-<MACHINE>.rootfs.tegraflash.tar.zst nvidia-deploy
	$ cd nvidia-deploy
	$ sudo tar --zstd -xvf wrlinux-image-std-sato-<MACHINE>.rootfs.tegraflash.tar.zst
	$ sudo ./l4t_flash_prerequisites.sh

5.2 Connect NVIDIA Orin Board with Host
---------------------------------------

Assume that the host system is Ubuntu.

Get the high quality USB Type-C cable shipped with NVIDIA Orin development kit, connect the
USB Type-C end to J40 of NVIDIA Orin AGX board, connect another end to PC host.

5.3 Force Board into Recovery Mode
----------------------------------

<1> Power off NVIDIA Orin AGX board by turning off power supply

<2> Press and hold down the Force Recovery button(S3), and then power on board by turning on power supply.

<3> (Optional) If the white LED(DS2) is not lit, press the Power button(S1).

<4> Release both buttons.

<5> Run below command on host to check whether NVIDIA Orin AGX board enters into recovery mode successfully.

    # lsusb
    ......
    Bus 003 Device 006: ID 0955:7023 NVIDIA Corp. APX
    ......

    If the host PC detect above device, it indicates that NVIDIA Orin AGX board enters into recovery mode successfully.

Note: user also can refer to below link with graphics introduction.
      https://developer.nvidia.com/embedded/learn/jetson-agx-orin-devkit-user-guide/howto.html#force-recovery-mode

5.4 Burn images into NVIDIA Orin board
--------------------------------------

    Burn images into eMMC with below commands:

    # cd nvidia-deploy
    # sudo ./doflash.sh

    After burning completely, there will be output as below:

    ......
    [ 269.0302 ] Flashing completed
    [ 269.0302 ] Coldbooting the device
    [ 269.0307 ] tegrarcm_v2 --chip 0x23 0 --ismb2
    [ 269.0312 ] MB2 version 01.00.0000
    [ 269.0330 ] Coldbooting the device
    [ 269.0334 ] tegrarcm_v2 --chip 0x23 0 --reboot coldboot
    [ 269.0338 ] MB2 version 01.00.0000


6. Boot Instructions
====================

6.1 Boot from Storage Sevice
----------------------------

6.1.1 Boot from internal eMMC
-----------------------------

1) Connect cables and external devices to NVIDIA Orin AGX board

   - Connect USB Type-C power supply with J24 of NVIDIA Orin AGX board.
   - Connect ethernet cable with J17 of NVIDIA Orin AGX board.
   - Connect DP monitor with J18 of NVIDIA Orin AGX board.
   - (Optional)Connect USB mouse and keyboard with J33 of NVIDIA Orin AGX board.

2) Turn on the power supply

3) Setup debug console
   - Check the debug serial port on host PC
     $ ls /dev/ttyACM*
     ttyACM0
     ttyACM1
     ttyACM2
     ttyACM3
     If there are 4 ttyACM* device nodes listed, it indicate host PC detects the debug serial port.

   - Setup debug serial port with below command:
     $ sudo minicom -s
     Enter the menu item of serial port setup, set the Serial Device as /dev/ttyACM0, and keep other
     configurations as default value.

4) Login WRLinux system
   <MACHINE> login: root
   root@<MACHINE>:~# uname -a
   Linux <MACHINE> 6.12.33-yocto-standard #1 SMP PREEMPT Fri Jun 20 01:52:55 UTC 2025 aarch64 GNU/Linux

6.2 Boot from Network
---------------------

Precondition 1: In order to boot from network environment, there must be a common DHCP server and setup
                tftp server and nfs server on their host PC.

Precondition 2: Because on NVIDIA Orin AGX platform(p3737-0000+p3701-0005), the UEFI firmware is not able to detect the on-board ethernet
                card, it needs to attach an USB-to-Ethernet converter to board.

6.2.1 Boot from tftp server
---------------------------

1) Deploy images on tftp server

   Copy <project path>/build/tmp/deploy/images/<MACHINE>/Image into boot directory of tftp server.
   Copy <project path>/build/tmp/deploy/images/<MACHINE>/devicetree/<$KERNEL_DEVICETREE>-bootable.dtb
   into directory of tftp server.

2) Deploy rootfs on nfs server

   Copy <project path>/build/tmp/deploy/images/<MACHINE>/wrlinux-image-std-sato-<MACHINE>.rootfs.tar.bz2
   into rootfs directory of nfs server.
   Decompress it with below command:
   tar jxvf wrlinux-image-std-sato-<MACHINE>.rootfs.tar.bz2

3) Enter UEFI shell environment

   Power on board, and then press 'ESC' key continuously to enter setup menu when debug serial port outputs below content.
   **********************************************************************
   Jetson System firmware version v36.4.3 date 2023-08-09T14:18:37+00:00
   ESC   to enter Setup.
   F11   to enter Boot Manager Menu.
   Enter to continue boot.
   **********************************************************************
   Enter 'Boot Manager' menu, and then select 'UEFI Shell' item.
   Enter boot partition with below command:
   Shell> ver
   UEFI Interactive Shell v2.2
   EDK II
   UEFI v2.70 (EDK II, 0x00010000)

4) Download images from tftp server

   Enter boot partition with below command:


   Shell> fs3:
   FS3:\> ls
   Directory of: FS3:\
   04/05/2011  23:00 <DIR>           512  EFI
             0 File(s)           0 bytes
             1 Dir(s)

   Note: If users install SD or NVME card on board, the partition number will change.

   Apply for IP address with below command:
   FS3:\> ifconfig -s eth0 dhcp
   FS3:\> ifconfig -l

   -----------------------------------------------------------------

   name         : eth0
   Media State  : Media present
   policy       : dhcp
   mac addr     : 00:E0:4C:68:3C:10

   ipv4 address : 192.168.3.110

   subnet mask  : 255.255.255.0

   default gateway: 192.168.3.1

     Routes (2 entries):
       Entry[0]
        Subnet : 192.168.3.0
        Netmask: 255.255.255.0
        Gateway: 0.0.0.0
       Entry[1]
       Subnet : 0.0.0.0
        Netmask: 0.0.0.0
        Gateway: 192.168.3.1

   DNS server   :
        192.168.3.1

   Download images with below command:
   FS3:\> tftp <tftp server ip> <tftp boot directory>/<$KERNEL_DEVICETREE>-bootable.dtb
   FS3:\> tftp <tftp server ip> <tftp boot directory>/Image

5) Boot system with NFS

   After downloading images from tftp server, boot system with below command:

   FS3:\> Image mminit_loglevel=4 console=ttyTCU0,115200 console=ttyAMA0,115200 firmware_class.path=/etc/firmware \
            fbcon=map:0 nospectre_bhb video=efifb:off console=tty0 root=/dev/nfs rw \
            nfsroot=<nfs server ip>:<nfs root path>,v3,tcp ip=dhcp bl_prof_dataptr=2031616@0x102C610000 \
            bl_prof_ro_ptr=65536@0x102C600000 dtb=./tegra234-p3737-0000+p3701-0005-nv-bootable.dtb

Note: KERNEL_DEVICETREE variable is defined in machine configuration file.

6.2.2 Boot from PXE server
--------------------------

About PXE boot mode, there is detailed descriptions on NVIDIA official documents, please refer to below link:
https://docs.nvidia.com/jetson/archives/r36.5/DeveloperGuide/SD/FlashingSupport.html#configuring-a-pxe-boot-server-for-uefi-bootloader-on-jetson
https://elinux.org/Jetson/L4T/peripheral/#PXE_boot


7. Features
===========

7.1 Power and Performance
-------------------------

7.1.1 Statistics Tool
---------------------

During development phase, it often needs to measure CPU, GPU, and memory usage status, so that make sure that SoC is able to support for
all the applications running on NVIDIA Orin platform. So, NVIDIA provides the statistics tool to show important metrics of Orin platform.

The "tegrastats" command is a tool that reports memory usage, GPU usage, CPU usage, and SOC temperature for NVIDIA Orin platform.
Run this command, It will start printing the system statistics each second. If users need to customize the tegrastats behavior, it provides
the following command-line options:

root@<MACHINE>:~# tegrastats --help
Usage: tegrastats [-option]
Options:
    --help                    : print this help screen
    --interval <millisec>     : sample the information in <milliseconds>
    --cpu_per_socket <numcpu> : display only the top <numcpu> by utilization per socket (default=6)
                                applicable only for socket-based systems
    --logfile  <filename>     : dump the output of tegrastats to <filename>
    --load_cfg <filename>     : load the information from <filename>
    --readall                 : collect all stats including performance intensive stats
    --save_cfg <filename>     : save the information to <filename>
    --start                 : run tegrastats as a daemon process in the background
    --stop                    : stop any running instances of tegrastats
    --verbose                 : print verbose message

There is an example of output printed by tegrastats running on Orin AGX platform.

07-04-2025 11:05:02  RAM 4000/62846MB (lfb 14352x4MB) CPU [12%@1497,4%@1497,100%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,0%@1497,7%@1497,0%@1497]
EMC_FREQ 6%@2133 GR3D_FREQ 29%@[815,815] NVENC off NVDEC 40%@115 NVJPG off NVJPG1 off VIC 56%@115 OFA off NVDLA0 off NVDLA1 off PVA0_FREQ off APE 174
cpu@51.843C soc2@48.562C soc0@49.031C gpu@49.187C tj@51.843C soc1@48.062C VDD_GPU_SOC 5187mW/5187mW VDD_CPU_CV 1596mW/1596mW VIN_SYS_5V0 4456mW/4476mW

There is an example of output printed by tegrastats running on Orin Nano platform.

09-23-2025 14:36:06 RAM 493/7624MB (lfb 1691x4MB) CPU [6%@729,4%@729,0%@729,0%@729,1%@729,2%@729]
EMC_FREQ 0%@2133 GR3D_FREQ 0%@[305] NVDEC off NVJPG off NVJPG1 off VIC off OFA off APE 200 cpu@48.656C
soc2@47.656C soc0@49.187C gpu@48.593C tj@50.531C soc1@50.531C VDD_IN 5453mW/5453mW VDD_CPU_GPU_CV 597mW/597mW VDD_SOC 1552mW/1552mW

Below description helps users to interpret some of the metrics provided by tegrastats.

Statistic                                   Description
RAM 4000/62846MB (lfb 15415x4MB)            4000 MB of RAM are in use, 62846MB MB of RAM is available for applications and there are 15415 blocks of size 4 MB
                                            the largest free block size) that can be allocated.
CPU [12%@1497,...,0%@1497]                  Each A@B value is a statistic for one of the CPUs. The first CPU in the example has a load of 12% and is running at 1497 MHz.
                                            The CPU load can also show as off if it's powered down.
EMC_FREQ 6%@2133                            6% of the External Memory Controller bandwidth is used, and it's running at 2133 MHz.
GR3D_FREQ 29%@[815,815]                     The first GPU engine has 29% of usage and is running at 815 MHz.
NVENC off                                   The NVIDIA Video Encoder Engine is in off status.
NVDEC 40%@115                               The NVIDIA Video Decoder Engine has 40% of usage and is running at 115 MHz, .
NVJPG off                                   The first NVIDIA JPEG Engine is in off status.
NVJPG1 off                                  The second NVIDIA JPEG Engine is in off status.
VIC 56%@115                                 The video image compositor has a 56% load and is running at 115 MHz.
OFA off                                     The Optical Flow Accelerator is in off status.
NVDLA0 off                                  The Deep Learning Accelerator 0 is in off status.
NVDLA1 off                                  The Deep Learning Accelerator 1 is in off status.
PVA0_FREQ off                               The Programmable Vision Accelerator is in off status.
APE 174                                     The Audio Processing Engine is running at 174 MHz.
cpu@51.843C                                 CPU with a temperature of 51.843 Celsius degrees.
gpu@49.187C                                 GPU with a temperature of 49.187 Celsius degrees.
VDD_GPU_SOC 5187mW/5187mW                   GPU SOC current power consumption is 5187 mW and the average power consumption is 5187 mW

7.1.2 Power Efficiency
----------------------

<1> Orin series module power mode

The Orin AGX module supports four different power modes known as 15W, 30W, and 50W.
For each mode, several configurations with various CPU and GPU frequencies, and a number of cores online are enabled.
Detailed configuration Orin AGX 64GB module as below:

Mode ID                            0               1               2               3
Power Budget                       EDP             15W             30W             50W
Online CPU                         12              4               8               12
CPU Max Frequency (MHz)            2201.6          1113.6          1728            1497.6
GPU TPC	                           8               3               4               6
GPU Max Frequency (MHz)            1301            408             612             816
DLA cores                          2               2               2               2
DLA Core Max Frequency (MHz)       1600            614.4           1369.6          1369.6
DLA Falcon Max frequency (MHz)     844.8           294.4           729.6           729.6
PVA Cores                          2               1               1               1
PVA VPS Max frequency (MHz)        1369.6          307.2           512             704
PVA AXI Max frequency (MHz)        985.6           217.6           358.4           486.4
Memory Max frequency (MHz)         3200            2133            3200            3200

The default mode is id 2, and Power Budget is 30W.
The EDP represents the upper limitation of Orin AGX module, so it has no power budget.

<2> Check current power mode

If users want to check the current power mode, please run below command:

root@<MACHINE>:~# /usr/sbin/nvpmodel -q
NV Power Mode: MODE_30W
2

<2> Change power mode

If users want to change mode of Orin AGX module, please run below command:
For example, change into mode 0.

root@<MACHINE>:~# /usr/sbin/nvpmodel -m 0
NVPM WARN: Golden image context is already created
NVPM WARN: Reboot required for changing to this power mode: 0
NVPM WARN: DO YOU WANT TO REBOOT NOW? enter YES/yes to confirm:
yes
NVPM WARN: rebooting..

Note: The mode persists across power cycles and won't change unless nvpmodel is called again.

7.1.3 Manage CPU State
----------------------

<1> List CPUs

The Orin platform always boot from one CPU denominated the master CPU which cannot be turned off. All other CPUs can be turned off.

For example, the CPUs are listed in the directory /sys/devices/system/cpu/ of Orin AGX platform as below:
root@<MACHINE>:~# ls -v /sys/devices/system/cpu | grep cpu[0-9]
cpu0
cpu1
cpu2
...

<2> Check the state of a CPU

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/online
1

<3> Turn on a CPU

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu2/online
Detected PIPT I-cache on CPU2
GICv3: CPU2: found redistributor 200 region 0:0x000000000f480000
CPU2: Booted secondary processor 0x0000000200 [0x410fd421]

<4> Turn off a CPU

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 0 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu2/online
psci: CPU2 killed (polled 0 ms)

7.1.4 Change CPU Frequency
--------------------------

<1> To show the CPU lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/cpuinfo_min_freq
115200
root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/cpuinfo_cur_freq
729600

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_available_frequencies
115200 192000 268800 345600 422400 499200 576000 652800 729600 806400 883200 960000 1036800 1113600 1190400 1267200 1344000
1420800 1497600 1574400 1651200 1728000 1804800 1881600 1958400 2035200 2112000 2188800 2201600

<3> To change the CPU upper boundary with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1728000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_max_freq

<4> To change the CPU lower boundary with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 192000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_min_freq

<5> To set the static CPU frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1728000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_min_freq
root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1728000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_max_freq

7.1.5 Manage GPU Frequency Scaling
----------------------------------

3D frequency scaling capability allows the NVIDIA Orin platform to change the GPU frequency according to
the load in order to reduce power consumption, 3D frequency scaling is enabled by default.

<1> To disable 3D frequency scaling with below command:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 0 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/enable_3d_scaling

<2> To enable 3D frequency scaling run the following command:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/enable_3d_scaling

7.1.6 Change GPU Frequency
--------------------------

<1> To show the GPU lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq
306000000
root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/cur_freq
306000000
root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq
1300500000

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/available_frequencies
306000000 408000000 510000000 612000000 714000000 816000000 918000000 1020000000 1122000000 1224000000 1300500000

<3> To change the GPU upper boundary with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1020000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq

<4> To change the GPU lower boundary with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 408000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq

<5> To set the static GPU frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1020000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq
root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1020000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq

7.1.7 Change Memory Frequency
-----------------------------

<1> To show the EMC lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/max_rate
3199000000
root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/min_rate
204000000
root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/rate
2133000000

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# ls /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/emc/tables/regular/
204000  2133000  3199000  665600

<3> To set static EMC frequency with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/mrq_rate_locked
root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/bwmgr/bwmgr_halt
root@<MACHINE>:~# echo 2133000000 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/rate

7.1.8 Jetson Clocks Tool
------------------------

NVIDIA provides the jetson_clocks.sh script to maximize Orin module performance by setting static max frequency to CPU, GPU, and EMC clocks.
The script can also be used to show current clock settings, store current clock settings into a file, and restore clock settings from a file.

root@<MACHINE>:~# jetson_clocks --help
Maximize jetson performance by setting static max frequency to CPU, GPU and EMC clocks.
Usage:
jetson_clocks [options]
  options,
  --help, -h         show this help message
  --show             display current settings
  --fan              set PWM fan speed to maximal
  --store [file]     store current settings to a file (default: ${HOME}/l4t_dfs.conf)
  --restore [file]   restore saved settings from a file (default: ${HOME}/l4t_dfs.conf)
  run jetson_clocks without any option to set static max frequency to CPU, GPU and EMC clocks.

<1> To show the current settings with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# jetson_clocks --show

<2> To store the current settings with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# jetson_clocks --store

<3> To maximize performance with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# jetson_clocks

<4> To restore the previous settings with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# jetson_clocks --restore

7.2 System Monitoring Tool
--------------------------

JTOP is a system monitoring tool specifically designed for NVIDIA Orin platform. It provides real-time information about
the platform's resource usage, including CPU, GPU, memory, disk, fan and engine. It also displays hardware temperatures, power consumption and frequency.
JTOP is a user-friendly command-line tool that helps users understand and optimize the performance of applications running on NVIDIA Orin platform.

<1> Run jtop tool

Check the IP address of NVIDIA Orin platform, and then login platform with ssh command:

	$ ssh root@<platform ip>

Set essential environment variable with below command:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# export TERM=xterm-256color

Run "jtop" command:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# jtop

After running the jtop tool, there are some tab items at the bottom of screen.

All Tab: It shows a comprehensive overview of your NVIDIA Orin platform's status and performance.
         It consolidates a wide range of information to give users a complete picture of their system's health
         and resource utilization.
GPU Tab: It shows processes running on the GPU, GPU memory utilization, GPU core utilization, and temperature.
CPU Tab: It shows the utilization and clock frequency of each CPU core.
Memory Tab: It shows memory utilization information and allows adding swap space.
Engine Tab: It shows the status of different hardware accelerator.
Control Tab: It is able to control over the fan, power modes, and jetson_clocks.
INFO Tab: It shows a centralized source of information about current NVIDIA Orin platform.

7.3 CUDA
--------

<1> CUDA Introduction

CUDA is a parallel computing platform that provides a framework for executing computations across thousands of parallel
processing cores within an NVIDIA GPU.
CUDA is a programming model that offers a programming interface for languages like C, C++, and Python, so that developers
could write code that can be executed in parallel on the GPU.

<2> Run CUDA sample

WRlinux has integrated CUDA libs and some samples so that users can develop and run their CUDA applications conveniently.
There are some cuda sample applications in directory /usr/bin/cuda-samples.
For example, user can run the "simplePrintf" application with below commands:

root@<MACHINE>:~# cd /usr/bin/cuda-samples
root@<MACHINE>:/usr/bin/cuda-samples# ./simplePrintf

GPU Device 0: "Ampere" with compute capability 8.7

Device 0: "Orin" with Compute 8.7 capability
printf() is called. Output:

[3, 0]:         Value is:10
......
[3, 7]:         Value is:10
[1, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[1, 7]:         Value is:10
[2, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[2, 7]:         Value is:10
[0, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[0, 7]:         Value is:10

The simplePrintf sample creates 32 threads running GPU cores parallel.

