the optee-os(BL32 image) is built automatically, and can be deployed into system referring to section 5.

7.14.2 OP-TEE Verificaton
-------------------------

Run below command to validate the OP-TEE feature with highest level 15

	# xtest -l 15

	Theoretically, all the test cases should be passed, and users will get below test result.

	55826 subtests of which 0 failed
	137 test cases of which 0 failed
	0 test cases were skipped
	TEE test application done!

7.15 System Redundancy
----------------------

NVIDIA Orin platform supports A/B redundant boot, the system maintains two copies (slots) of bootloader
firmware and the root filesystem, allowing automatic rollback if one of 2 slots boots up failed.

7.15.1 A/B Slot Layout
----------------------

The NVIDIA Orin platform uses a shadow copy mechanism to enable redundant boot. Each firmware component in
the boot chain is stored in two copies, commonly referred to as Slot A and Slot B. At boot time, the Boot ROM
and subsequent boot stages select the correct slot based on runtime state, ensuring the system boots from a
known-good copy. The one exception is the BR-BCT (Boot ROM Boot Configuration Table), which exists as a single
instance at a fixed storage location. The BR-BCT serves as the root entry for the Boot ROM and contains pointers
to both Slot A and Slot B, enabling the A/B selection for all subsequent components.

This two-slot implementation is as follows:

	┌──────────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────────┐
	│      Slot A (0)      │  │      Slot B (1)      │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  A_mb1               │  │  B_mb1               │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  ......              │  │  ......              │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  A_mb2               │  │  B_mb2               │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  ......              │  │  ......              │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  A_cpu-bootloader    │  │  B_cpu-bootloader    │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  ......              │  │  ......              │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  A_kernel            │  │  B_kernel            │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  A_kernel-dtb        │  │  B_kernel-dtb        │
	├──────────────────────┤  ├──────────────────────┤
	│  APP (rootfs A)      │  │  APP_b (rootfs B)    │
	└──────────────────────┘  └──────────────────────┘

7.15.2 Boot Control
-------------------

nvbootctrl is the userspace CLI tool for managing A/B redundant boot slots on the NVIDIA Orin platform.
It can be used to dump the slot information, set the active slot and so on. Detailed information as below:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl
	nvbootctrl - command-line wrapper for the boot_control HAL.

	Usage:
	  nvbootctrl [Options] Command

	Options:
	  -t <target>                    - target available: bootloader or rootfs. Default: bootloader.

	Commands:
	  get-number-slots               - Prints number of slots.
	  get-current-slot               - Prints currently running SLOT.
	  set-active-boot-slot SLOT      - On next boot, load and execute SLOT.
	  dump-slots-info                - Prints info for slots.
	  verify                         - Verify the bootloader and rootfs boot.
	  verify-bl                      - Verify the bootloader boot only.
	  is-rootfs-ab-enabled           - Rootfs only. Return 0 if rootfs A/B is disabled;
	                                 - Return 1 if rootfs A/B is enabled, current slot is A;
	                                 - Return 2 if rootfs A/B is enabled, current slot is B;

	SLOT parameter is the zero-based slot-number.

Note: When running nvbootctrl command with is-rootfs-ab-enabled to check whether rootfs A/B is enabled,
      it needs to echo the return value explicitly as below, because nvbootctrl doesn't show any information
      in shell terminal windows.
      root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl -t rootfs is-rootfs-ab-enabled; echo $?

<1>. Switch Boot Slot

Run below command to check current running slot.

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl get-current-slot
    0

Run below command to set active slot for next boot, and then reboot.

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl set-active-boot-slot 1
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

During system restarts, some log information as below can indicate system boots up from slot 1.

	[0000.068] I> MB1 (version: 1.4.0.7-t234-54845784-56c609fa)
	......
	[0000.327] I> Loading MEMBCT
	[0000.329] I> Slot: 1
	......
	[0001.854] I> Loading MB2
	[0001.856] I> Slot: 1
	......
	I> MB2 (version: 0.0.0.0-t234-54845784-6ee03b4e)
	......
	I> Partition name: B_spe-fw
	......

After system boots up completely, check current running slot with below command.

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl get-current-slot
    1

After system boots up completely, check current mounted rootfs with below command.

	root@<MACHINE>:~# blkid
	......
	/dev/mmcblk0p2: UUID="a98903c5-2813-4daf-b44b-0bd3a1b03bfe" BLOCK_SIZE="4096" TYPE="ext4" PARTLABEL="APP_b" PARTUUID="69351178-fb29-46c1-aac5-e50f3a492752"
	......
	/dev/mmcblk0p1: UUID="a98903c5-2813-4daf-b44b-0bd3a1b03bfe" BLOCK_SIZE="4096" TYPE="ext4" PARTLABEL="APP" PARTUUID="09600655-fc34-4684-9fe1-462fc8d8177c"
	......

	root@<MACHINE>:~# mount
	......
	/dev/mmcblk0p2 on / type ext4 (rw,relatime)

<2>. Failover Boot

Run below command to makw sure system boots up from slot 0

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl get-current-slot
	0

Destrory B_kernel and APP_b partitons with below command

	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/B_kernel bs=1M count=100
	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/APP_b bs=1M count=100

Run below command to select slot 1 for next boot, and restart system
	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl set-active-boot-slot 1
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

Because B_kernel and APP_b partitons are corrupted, system boots up failed, bootloader will try 3 times,
and then switch to slot 0 to boot system normally.

After system boots up normally, check the boot slot with below command

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl get-current-slot
	0

7.16 System Update
------------------

The NVIDIA Orin platforms support a comprehensive system update framework based on the A/B redundancy architecture.
The system maintains two complete sets of boot partitions(Slot A and Slot B), allowing safe updates to the inactive
slot while the active slot continues running.

Take NVIDIA Orin AGX platform as an example, in order to update the full system, user needs to follow the instructions
in the next three chapters step by step.

7.16.1 Update kernel image and dtb
----------------------------------

In general, the UEFI L4T Launcher boots up system with extlinux.conf, which specifies the kernel path on the rootfs.
In this section, the kernel image to be updated is located in raw kernel(A_kernel/B_kernel) partitions. It is used as
fallback if the one on rootfs are corrupted. Detailed update steps as below:

<1>. Get kernel image and dtb

	User can get the kernel image and dtb in <project-path>/build/tmp/build/tmp/deploy/images/nvidia-orin-agx/
	kernel image: tegra-minimal-initramfs-nvidia-orin-agx.cboot
	dtb: devicetree/tegra234-p3737-0000+p3701-0005-nv.dtb

<2>. Copy kernel image and dtb to target platform

	Example command as below:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# scp <user name>@<host ip>:<host path>/tegra234-p3737-0000+p3701-0005-nv.dtb /root
	root@<MACHINE>:~# scp <user name>@<host ip>:<host path>/tegra-minimal-initramfs-nvidia-orin-agx.cboot /root
	root@<MACHINE>:~# sync

<3>. Burn kernel image and dtb

	If system boots up from slot 0, user can burn B_kernel and B_kernel-dtb with below commands:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/root/tegra-minimal-initramfs-nvidia-orin-agx.cboot of=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/B_kernel bs=4M
	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/root/tegra234-p3737-0000+p3701-0005-nv.dtb of=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/B_kernel-dtb bs=4M
	root@<MACHINE>:~# sync

<4>. Verify kernel image and dtb

	It recommends that verify the fallback kernel image and dtb after rootfs is also updated, because kernel modules
	need to match with reasonable kernel version.

	After burning kernel image and dtb, switch slot 1 as the booting slot for next time.
	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl set-active-boot-slot 1
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

	After system boots up from slot 1, remove kernel image and boot configuration file from rootfs. Only in this way,
	the fallback kernel image and dtb will be used in next boot.
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cd /boot
	root@<MACHINE>:~# mv Image Image-6.18.20-yocto-standard extlinux/extlinux.conf /root
	root@<MACHINE>:~# sync
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

	During system boots up from slot 1 again, check whether fallback kernel and dtb are loaded with boot log.
	After system boots up from slot 1 again, check the kernel version and dtb specific node to make sure that
	the fallback kernel and dtb are loaded normally.
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cat /proc/version
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cd /proc/device-tree
	root@<MACHINE>:~# ls -laR

	Restore kernel image and boot configuration so that system boots up with primary configuration.
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cd /root
	root@<MACHINE>:~# mv Image Image-6.18.20-yocto-standard /boot
	root@<MACHINE>:~# mv extlinux.conf /boot/extlinux
	root@<MACHINE>:~# sync

	Switch slot 0 as the booting slot for next time.
	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl set-active-boot-slot 0
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

7.16.2 Update rootfs
--------------------

There are 2 partitions(APP and APP_b) used to store rootfs. Detailed steps as below:

<1>. Get rootfs

	After building project, the rootfs image is located at
	<project-path>/build/tmp/deploy/images/nvidia-orin-agx/wrlinux-image-std-sato-nvidia-orin-agx.ext4

<2>. Copy rootfs to target platform

	Example command as below:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# scp <user name>@<host ip>:<host path>/wrlinux-image-std-sato-nvidia-orin-agx.rootfs.ext4 /tmp

<3>. Burn rootfs image

	If system boots up from slot 0, user can burn APP_b with below commands:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/tmp/wrlinux-image-std-sato-nvidia-orin-agx.rootfs.ext4 of=/dev/disk/by-partlabel/APP_b bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync

<4>. Verify rootfs

	After burning rootfs, switch slot 1 as the booting slot for next time.

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl set-active-boot-slot 1
	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

	After system boots up from slot 1 again, check the specific content.

7.16.3 Update bootloader
------------------------

For NVIDIA Orin platform, bootloader image includes MB1, MB2, UEFI, BPMP, OP-TEE, and other co-processor firmware stored on QSPI NOR flash.
It is updated with UEFI Capsule update mechanism that maintains two sets of bootloader partitions on the QSPI NOR flash. The bootloader
capsule file(tegra-bl.cap) is generated by tegra-uefi-capsules recipe. In order to update bootloader, The capsule file should be placed
on the ESP partition under EFI/UpdateCapsule/ directory, and then use OsIndications EFI variable to trigger UEFI to process the capsule
file on the next boot. This is the only method available for QSPI firmware updates, as the QSPI flash is protected by a hardware firewall
and is not accessible from the Linux kernel.

If system boots up from slot 0, detailed update steps as below:

<1>. Deploy bootloader capsule file to ESP partition

	root@<MACHINE>:~# mkdir -p /boot/efi/EFI/UpdateCapsule
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cp /opt/nvidia/UpdateCapsule/tegra-bl.cap /boot/efi/EFI/UpdateCapsule/
	root@<MACHINE>:~# sync

<2>. Trigger UEFI to process the capsule file

	Set OsIndications EFI variable

	Option1:
	root@<MACHINE>:~# cd /sys/firmware/efi/efivars/
	root@<MACHINE>:~# printf "\x07\x00\x00\x00\x04\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00" > /tmp/var_tmp.bin
	root@<MACHINE>:~# dd if=/tmp/var_tmp.bin of=OsIndications-8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c bs=12;sync

	Option2:
	root@<MACHINE>:~# oe4t-set-uefi-OSIndications

	Restart system

	root@<MACHINE>:~# reboot

<3>. Verify bootloader update

	During system boots up, UEFI will update firmware images into QSPI flash, and then switch to slot 1 automatically.
	After a successful capsule update, the nv_update_verifier service runs nvbootctrl verify command to check the capsule update
	status, confirm the new firmware boots up successfully and mark boots slot as good. If the new firmware fails to boot, UEFI
	will roll back to the previous slot automatically.
	The capsule update status can be checked with below command:

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl get-current-slot
	1

	root@<MACHINE>:~# nvbootctrl dump-slots-info
	Current version: 36.5.0
	Capsule update status: 1
	Current bootloader slot: B
	Active bootloader slot: B
	num_slots: 2
	slot: 0,             status: normal
	slot: 1,             status: normal

	About Capsule update status, detailed meaning as below:
	0: No capsule update performed
	1: Capsule update successful - firmware updated and booted from new slot
	2: Capsule installed successfully but boot from new firmware failed - rolled back to previous slot
	3: Capsule installation failed - firmware was not written


